{
  "$comment": "VAPP-120 typed host ACTIONS ×4: every Exponential MCP operation is a host function an app may call, `exponential.<noun>.<op>` (the apps noun's ops included; the `exponential_sessions_send` verb tool = `exponential_sessions_send`; `session_self` and `guide_write` are not offered: they need an agent's session), run by the client's app host as a tools/call on the instance's MCP as the viewer (the `harness_mcp` transport, arguments = {op, …args}; no `op` for the verb tool). A package lists what it calls in `functions` (an exact name or a `prefix*` pattern: `exponential_issues_*`); the SDK denies anything else. GRANTS (the 2026-10-24 ruling, a conservative default): only a team OWNER (`grantRole`; `canGrantActions(role)`) sets or changes an app's grants, the app's creator gets no grant power from creating it; a DESTRUCTIVE operation (`destructiveWords`: a word of the tool name or op is delete, merge, close, end, remove, trash; `actionDestructive`, `actionGrantable` = not destructive) can NEVER be granted: the server refuses it, every host ignores a stored grant for it (`grantAllows` is false), and it always shows the consent card with the operation's words, for every viewer; the settings row of a destructive action shows `copy.tools.always` in place of the switch, a non-owner sees the switches disabled. `grantableCases` and `canGrantCases` replay ×4 beside `grantCases` (which carry: an owner's grant skips the card for a non-destructive op, a destructive op ignores a grant). A DESTRUCTIVE CALL (`destructiveArgs`: the operations prompts.json flags destructive that ride on an argument of a grantable action: `exponential_sessions_send` with `stop` set = the `stop-run` prompt) ignores a grant the same way and shows the destructive card (`callDestructive(name, args)`; `grantAllows(granted, name, args)` takes the call's args, an arg present and not false/null counts); the action itself stays grantable for its other calls. `apps.granted_tools` (synced on the apps shape, a list of exact function names) = what the app may run as the viewer WITHOUT asking: the app's SETTINGS page (web + desktop; phones have no app settings) shows a `copy.tools` group under the records line with one row per declared function (`declaredActions`: patterns expand against `functions`), the display row's title (contract `expToolDisplay`, `<noun>_<op>`) + the function name muted + a Switch (an owner; others see it disabled; a destructive action shows `copy.tools.always` instead), `copy.tools.none` when the package declares none, `copy.tools.hint` under the title. The server (`apps.update`, an owner only) accepts a grant only for a non-destructive action the HEAD package declares, and every new version (upsert, restore) drops the grants it no longer declares: a grant is a choice about the current package, never inherited by a later version that re-declares the action; a version written by anyone but a team OWNER (a member's upsert or restore) clears ALL the grants, so a non-owner's code never runs on an owner's grant without the consent card, while an owner's own new version keeps the grants it still declares. A typed call's `op` is the NAME's: an `op` key in the args never changes the operation (×4). CONSENT: a declared, ungranted call shows the consent card worded for the operation (`actionConsent`: title `Allow this surface to <title, lower-cased><subject>?` where subject = the arg named by the row's `subjectKey` when it is a string; the destructive variant when a word of the tool name or op is delete, merge, close, end, remove, trash; the arguments block = the args without `op`), Allow runs it ONCE; a granted call runs without a card; `harness_mcp` keeps the generic copy and always asks. The TS reference `app-actions.ts` is replayed by desktop `domain::app_actions`, iOS `AppActions.swift`, Android `AppActions.kt` (`parseCases`, `grantCases`, `declaredCases`, `consentCases`); `functions` is the ×4 list (the web gates it against its noun table). VAPP-158 OWNER ACTIONS (`ownerActions`, `actionOwnerOnly`): the non-destructive operations the server runs for a team OWNER only (adding a member, minting or revoking an invite, adding an MCP server, creating or editing a prompt, changing the team's settings) are never grantable either: `actionGrantable` is false, the server refuses the grant, every host ignores a stored one (`grantAllows` false) and the call always shows the consent card (the plain card, not the destructive variant: `callDestructive` is unchanged), so an app never runs an owner's operation card-free for anyone, owners included; the settings row shows `copy.tools.always` like a destructive one. `ownerOnlyCases` replay ×4. EXP-1657 ALIASES (`aliases`, old name → new name): the retired `exponential_team_config` tool split into its nouns (labels, statuses, prompts, agents, mcp_servers, repositories; `mcp_servers_add` = `mcp_servers.create`, `mcp_servers_remove` = `mcp_servers.delete`, `repositories_add` = `repositories.create`), so `exponential.team_config.<x>_<y>` became `exponential.<noun>.<op>`. An already-published package keeps working: every host registers each alias as a function too (`actionAliases`), an alias PARSES to its new name's target, and everything else follows the NEW name (`canonicalAction`): destructive, owner-only, grantable, the display title and the consent card; a grant matches when the grant and the call name the same canonical action (a grant stored under an old name counts for the new one and vice versa); `declaredActions` expands a package's names and patterns over the aliases too and returns their NEW names. Aliases are never listed in `functions`, `tools` or a declared list. Alias cases sit in `parseCases`, `grantCases`, `grantableCases`, `ownerOnlyCases`, `declaredCases` and `consentCases` (`$why` names them).",
